<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tartu (Russian–Estonian)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1920</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Tartu was signed on 2 February 1920 between the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a>. Russia recognised Estonia's independence in perpetuity and agreed a border that gave Estonia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a> and Petseri. It was the first international recognition of Estonia, which regards the treaty as a foundation of its statehood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tartu-russian-estonian.jpg" alt="Treaty of Tartu (Russian–Estonian)" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Armin Lomp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joffe_signing_the_Treaty_of_Tartu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
